title: Setup guide
---
body:

This guide gets you ready to run the course notebooks in VS Code using a conda‑forge environment managed with **mamba**.

#### Install Miniforge
- Go to <https://conda-forge.org/download/>.
- Download the Miniforge installer for your OS and CPU.
- Run the installer with defaults.

#### Install VS Code
- Download and install from <https://code.visualstudio.com/>.

#### Download the course materials
- Direct download (recommended): <https://github.com/yoavram/ModelsPopBiol/archive/refs/heads/master.zip>
  - Unzip and note the folder path (e.g., `~/Downloads/ModelsPopBiol-master`).
- If you prefer `git`, clone <https://github.com/yoavram/ModelsPopBiol> (not covered here).

#### Open the course folder in VS Code
- Start VS Code in the course folder: **File → Open Folder…** → select the course folder.

#### Install the Python extension for VS Code
- Open VS Code → Extensions → search **Python** (by Microsoft) → Install.
- Reference: <https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/python/python-quick-start>

#### Install the Jupyter extension for VS Code
- Extensions → search **Jupyter** (by Microsoft) → Install.
- Reference: <https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/datascience/jupyter-notebooks>


#### Create the course environment from the VS Code terminal
- In VS Code: **Terminal → New Terminal**. Confirm the terminal path is the course folder (the one containing `environment.yaml`).
- Run:
  ```bash
  mamba env create -f environment.yaml
  conda activate modelspopbio
  ```
- If `mamba` is unavailable, install it with `conda install -n base -c conda-forge mamba` or run `conda env create -f environment.yaml` instead.

#### Open the notebooks and select the kernel
- Open `index.ipynb` in VS Code.
- In the notebook editor, set the kernel to the course environment:
  - Click the kernel picker at the top right → choose the interpreter named `modelspopbio`.

#### Quick test
In a notebook cell, run:
```python
import numpy
print(numpy.__version__)
```
You should see version number without errors.

#### Enable GitHub Copilot in VS Code
- Apply or sign in at <https://education.github.com/pack> to get Copilot Pro for free.
- Set up **GitHub Copilot** in VS Code: <https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/setup>
